<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amarna letters</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> letters are a set of clay tablets from the 14th century BCE, discovered at Amarna in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, recording diplomatic correspondence between Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> and other rulers. Written mostly in Akkadian, the international language of the time, they include letters from the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and Levantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. They show the role of the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> in a network of great powers and the lasting influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-language">Akkadian language</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amarna-letters.jpg" alt="Amarna letters" width="320" height="313" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Five_Amarna_letters_on_display_at_the_British_Museum,_LondonA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
